  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Complex conjugation & division"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "When dividing ***complex*** numbers, we can use the **complex conjugate** to make the denominator a real number, which makes carrying out the division much easier."
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a complex conjugate?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- For a given complex number $z=a+bi$**, the complex conjugate of **$z$ **is denoted as **$z^{*}$, where $z^{*}=a-bi$\n- If $z=a-bi$ then $z^{*}=a+bi$\n- You will find that:\n\n  - $z+z^{*}$ is always real because $(a+bi)+(a-bi)=2a$\n  \n    - For example: $(6+5i)+(6-5i)=6+6+5i-5i=12$\n  - $z-z^{*}$ is always imaginary because `open parentheses a plus b straight i close parentheses minus left parenthesis a minus b straight i right parenthesis equals 2 b straight i`\n  \n    - For example: $(6+5i)-(6-5i)=6-6+5i-(-5i)=10i$\n  - $z\\timesz^{*}$ is always real because `open parentheses a plus b straight i close parentheses open parentheses a minus b straight i close parentheses equals a squared plus a b straight i minus a b straight i minus b squared straight i squared equals a squared plus b squared` (as $i^{2}=-1$)\n  \n    - For example: $(6+5i)(6-5i)=36+30i--30i-25i^{2}=36--25(-1)=61$"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "** **"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I divide complex numbers?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- When we divide complex numbers, we can express the calculation in the form of a fraction, and then **start by multiplying the top and bottom by the *****conjugate***** of the denominator**:\n\n  - $\\frac{a+bi}{c+di}=\\frac{a+bi}{c+di}\\times\\frac{c-di}{c-di}$\n- This ensures we are multiplying by 1; so not affecting the overall value\n- This gives us a real number as the denominator because we have a complex number multiplied by its conjugate ($zz^{\\ast}$)\n- This process is very similar to “rationalising the denominator” with surds"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "![8-1-2-dividing-complex-numbers](assets/39a09dbb434f-8-1-2-dividing-complex-numbers.png)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- We can speed up the process for finding $zz\\ast$by using the basic pattern of `open parentheses x plus a close parentheses open parentheses x minus a close parentheses equals x squared minus a squared`\n- We can apply this to complex numbers: `open parentheses a plus b straight i close parentheses open parentheses a minus b straight i close parentheses equals a squared minus b squared straight i squared equals a squared plus b squared`\n(using the fact that $i^{2}=-1$)\n- So $3+4i$ multiplied by its conjugate would be $3^{2}+4^{2}=25$"
  }
 ]
}